@page "/"
@inject CharacterSession Session
@inject ICharacterStore Store
@inject IJSRuntime Js

<PageTitle>Tier — Prowlers &amp; Paragons</PageTitle>

<h1>Choose a tier</h1>
<p class="muted">
    The tier sets the Hero Point budget and the Trait Cap — the highest rank any single
    Trait may reach. Everything after this is measured against those two numbers.
</p>

@* **The two sample characters used to be here and are now in the portfolio.** They are a
   demonstration — a way to see what a finished sheet looks like without building one — and a
   demonstration is not a step in making your own character. Somebody who has come here to build
   one had to walk past them first.

   What stays is the one control that belongs to building: throwing this character away. *@
<Panel Title="Starting over" Aside="There is no undo" NoPrint="true">
    <p class="small muted">
        Whatever you build is remembered in this browser, so you can close the tab and come back
        to it. It is not sent anywhere and it is only on this device — download the sheet from the
        last step if you want a copy that travels.
    </p>

    @* Asks first, in the page rather than in a browser dialogue, and only when there is
       something to lose. On a fresh sheet it acts on one click, which is the visit where nobody
       has anything at stake. *@
    <div class="inline">
        @if (_pending is { } pending)
        {
            <button type="button" class="btn danger" @onclick="() => Do(pending)">
                Yes, @pending.Verb
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="btn" @onclick="() => _pending = null">
                Keep this character
            </button>
        }
        else
        {
            <button type="button" class="btn danger" @onclick="() => Ask(Discard())">
                Start a new character
            </button>
            <NavLink class="btn" href="portfolio">Or start from a finished one</NavLink>
        }
    </div>
</Panel>

@* Six cards rather than six full-width rows. These are the one set of choices on the
   whole app a reader is meant to compare against each other rather than search through,
   which is why this list alone turns its filter box off — and why each card carries its
   consequence, the budget and the cap, as a line of its own under the description. *@
<OptionList Class="cards" Filterable="false">
    @foreach (var tier in Session.Rules.Tiers)
    {
        var tierId = tier.Id;
        <OptionRow Name="@tier.Name"
                   Caveat="@Consequence(tier)"
                   Cost="@($"{tier.HeroPoints} Hero Points · Trait Cap {tier.TraitCapRank}d")"
                   Selected="@(Session.Sheet.SelectedTierId == tierId)"
                   OnSelect="() => SelectTier(tierId)">
            <NameExtra>
                @if (Session.Sheet.SelectedTierId == tierId)
                {
                    <span class="tag own">Selected</span>
                }
            </NameExtra>
        </OptionRow>
    }
</OptionList>

@* The sandbox, put where the budget is introduced rather than in the chrome — the tier is what
   sets the figure, so this is where switching it off makes sense.

   **It is not the Hero/Villain switch and must not become one again.** Ch.9 builds Villains by
   exactly the Hero rules, so "no budget" was never a fact about Villains: it is a GM building to
   whatever the scene needs, which a Hero campaign does too. Keeping them apart means a Villain
   can be held to a tier's points and a Hero need not be. *@
<Panel Title="Hero Point limit" Aside="Optional">
    <p class="small muted">
        The tier's Hero Points are a budget by default and the review step reports going over
        it. Switch that off to build without a limit — the spend is still tracked and shown as
        a running total, and everything else is still checked: Trait Caps, flaw counts, and
        options that cannot go where they have been put.
    </p>

    <Field Class="inline">
        @* aria-pressed as a string, for the reason recorded in CLAUDE.md: Blazor renders a true
           bool as an empty attribute, which is invalid ARIA and announces the opposite. *@
        <button type="button" class="btn"
                aria-pressed="@(Session.UnlimitedBudget ? "true" : "false")"
                @onclick="() => Session.UnlimitedBudget = !Session.UnlimitedBudget">
            @(Session.UnlimitedBudget ? "Building without a limit" : "Hold me to the tier's budget")
        </button>

        @* The paragraph above already says what this does — the tip adds the rulebook's reason,
           which is the sort of thing worth having available and not worth spending a paragraph
           on. Nothing here is the only place anything is said. *@
        <Tooltip Term="building without a limit"
                 Text="Ch.9 builds Villains by exactly the Hero rules, so no budget was never a fact about Villains — it is a GM building to whatever the scene needs. Either kind of character can be built either way." />
    </Field>
</Panel>

<Panel Title="Starting package" Aside="Optional">
    <p class="small muted">
        A package buys the ranks it grants, at a small discount — the Superhero Package is
        50 HP for what would cost 54 bought rank by rank. You can still buy higher ranks on
        top; only the ranks above the package's own are charged again.
    </p>

    <ChosenList>
        <ChosenRow>
            <Body>
                <div><b>No package</b></div>
                <div class="small muted">Buy every rank yourself.</div>
            </Body>
            <Actions>
                <button type="button" class="btn small"
                        disabled="@(Session.Sheet.SelectedPackageId is null)"
                        @onclick="() => SelectPackage(null)">
                    @(Session.Sheet.SelectedPackageId is null ? "Current" : "Choose")
                </button>
            </Actions>
        </ChosenRow>
        @foreach (var pkg in Session.Rules.CreationRules.OptionalPackages)
        {
            var chosen = Session.Sheet.SelectedPackageId == pkg.Id;
            <ChosenRow Cost="@($"{pkg.Cost} HP")">
                <Body>
                    <div>
                        <b>@pkg.Name</b>
                        <span class="muted small">
                            — @(pkg.AbilitiesRank)d in every Ability, @(pkg.TalentsRank)d in every Talent
                        </span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="small muted">@pkg.Description</div>
                </Body>
                <Actions>
                    <button type="button" class="btn small" disabled="@chosen"
                            @onclick="() => SelectPackage(pkg.Id)">
                        @(chosen ? "Current" : "Choose")
                    </button>
                </Actions>
            </ChosenRow>
        }
    </ChosenList>
</Panel>

<StepButtons Next="characteristics" NextLabel="Buy characteristics"
             NextDisabled="@(Session.Sheet.SelectedTierId is null)" />

@code {
    private Replacement? _pending;

    /// <summary>Something that replaces the character, and what to call it when asking.</summary>
    private sealed record Replacement(string Verb, Func<Task> Run);

    private Replacement Discard() => new("discard this character", async () =>
    {
        Session.StartAgain();

        // Forget the stored copy too. Clearing only the session left the old character in
        // local storage, so it came back on the next visit — which reads as the button not
        // having worked, on the control where that matters most.
        //
        // After emptying the sheet, not before: emptying it fires the write-through save,
        // and the clear has to land second. In WebAssembly the save's interop call against a
        // synchronous JS function completes before StartAgain returns, so it does. If that
        // ever stopped holding — a storage API that returns a promise — the worst case is a
        // stored empty character, which restores to exactly what this button produces.
        await Store.ClearAsync();
    });

    /// <summary>Asks first, unless the sheet is empty and there is nothing to ask about.</summary>
    private async Task Ask(Replacement replacement)
    {
        if (Session.HasSomethingToLose) _pending = replacement;
        else await replacement.Run();
    }

    private async Task Do(Replacement replacement)
    {
        _pending = null;
        await replacement.Run();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What choosing this tier means, in one line.
    ///
    /// <para><b>The description alone, and the note deliberately not appended to it.</b> Iconic
    /// is the only tier that carries a note, and its description already says the thing the note
    /// exists to say — "The 200 HP listed is a bare minimum for this tier." Concatenating the two
    /// printed that point twice in one paragraph, and because the cards are an equal-height grid
    /// row, the five-line Iconic card left High Level and Legendary with about five lines of dead
    /// white above their cost rule. An earlier version of this comment claimed the note was
    /// needed or the card would state a budget the rulebook does not; the description states it.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private static string Consequence(TierModel tier) => tier.Description;

    private void SelectTier(string id)
    {
        Session.Sheet.SelectedTierId = id;
        Session.NotifyChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Applying a package sets every Ability and Talent it grants to its rank, unless the
    /// character has already bought higher. Removing one leaves those ranks alone — they
    /// are the character's now, and silently deleting a player's choices to undo a
    /// discount would lose work rather than save Hero Points.
    /// </summary>
    private void SelectPackage(string? id)
    {
        Session.Sheet.SelectedPackageId = id;

        if (id is not null &&
            Session.Rules.CreationRules.OptionalPackages.FirstOrDefault(p => p.Id == id) is { } pkg)
        {
            foreach (var ability in Session.Rules.Abilities)
                if (Session.Sheet.GetAbilityRank(ability.Id) < pkg.AbilitiesRank)
                    Session.Sheet.AbilityRanks[ability.Id] = pkg.AbilitiesRank;

            foreach (var talent in Session.Rules.Talents)
                if (Session.Sheet.GetTalentRank(talent.Id) < pkg.TalentsRank)
                    Session.Sheet.TalentRanks[talent.Id] = pkg.TalentsRank;
        }

        Session.NotifyChanged();
    }
}
